/*  A lifetime you wrote yourself, and fields you did not have to fill in.

    ★★ `and region` says this object OWNS A LIFETIME. Pulling it opens a scope that its `Done.`
    closes, exactly as a rabbit's does — and it can be told to `bury`. That is the whole claim:
    the thing a rabbit does is a thing the language offers, not a power one built-in type was
    given. `Prelude/rabbit.cufe` says `and region` like this file does, so a rabbit is the first
    instance of the rule rather than an exception to it.

    Compare `stashes.cufe`, which buries from `the rabbit helper`. This buries from
    `the workshop bench` — a type a stranger could write and publish — and nothing else changes.

    ★ A book is a module that owns NO lifetime; a region is a module that owns one. Same
    mechanism, and the surface says which: you consult a book, and you open a workshop.

    ★★ FIELD DEFAULTS are the other half. `with default` lets a construction site name only what
    differs, which is what makes a type with many settings bearable. The default is filled in AT
    EACH SITE, not computed once at the definition — so a defaulted series would give every
    object its own, and the mutable-default trap is answered by construction rather than by a
    rule. `memo` below names one field of three.

    ⚠ Values made inside a region may not be stored where they would outlive it. The rule is the
    rabbit's rule because it is one set of rules.
*/

Define object workshop with () and region.

Define object job with (the text title,
                        the number copies with default 1,
                        the text finish with default "plain").

/// Hands out ticket numbers for as long as anyone keeps asking.
///
/// ★ Nothing in the declaration says "stash" — a body that buries anywhere hands back a
/// `stash of <what it buries>`, the same way `return a failure` decides fallibility. What it
/// does need is something that owns a lifetime to do the burying, so it takes one.
Bind number to tickets-from, given (the workshop bench, the number first-ticket):
    Define next as first-ticket.
    Repeat:
        Have bench bury next.
        The next becomes next + 1.
    Until false.
Done.

State "the shop opens".

Pull workshop as floor.
    Define tickets as cast tickets-from on (floor, 100).

    Define memo as a new job { the title "memo" }.
    Define poster as a new job { the title "poster", the copies 25, the finish "gloss" }.

    State "ticket {unbury tickets but void is 0}: {memo's title}, {memo's copies}, {memo's finish}".
    State "ticket {unbury tickets but void is 0}: {poster's title}, {poster's copies}, {poster's finish}".
Done.

State "the shop closes, and everything made on the floor goes with it".
